1 DEASSIGN

   Deletes a logical name, or every logical name of a table.

   Format

     DEASSIGN logical-name[:]
     DEASSIGN/ALL [logical-name-table-qualifier]

2 Parameter

 logical-name[:]

   The name to delete.  One trailing colon is removed, so to delete a
   name that ends in a colon, give two.  With /ALL, no name is given.

2 Description

   Deleting a table's name from LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY or
   LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY deletes the table and every table below it.

2 Qualifiers
/ALL

   Deletes every name of the table at the access mode and any less
   privileged mode.

/EXECUTIVE_MODE

   Deletes names at executive mode and any less privileged mode.

/GROUP

   Deletes from the group table, LNM$GROUP.

/JOB

   Deletes from the job table, LNM$JOB.

/[NO]LOG

   Accepted for VMS's sake; DEASSIGN reports nothing either way.

/PROCESS

   Deletes from the process table, LNM$PROCESS (the default).

/SUPERVISOR_MODE

   Deletes names at supervisor mode and user mode (the default), so the
   executive-mode names SYS$INPUT, SYS$OUTPUT, SYS$ERROR, and
   SYS$COMMAND are left alone.

/SYSTEM

   Deletes from the system table, LNM$SYSTEM.

/TABLE=name

   Deletes from the table named.

/USER_MODE

   Deletes names at user mode only.

2 Examples

   1.$ DEASSIGN WORK

     Deletes the process table's WORK.

   2.$ DEASSIGN/TABLE=LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY TAX

     Deletes the table TAX and every table below it.

   3.$ DEASSIGN/JOB/ALL

     Deletes every supervisor-mode and user-mode name of the job table.
